  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the minimum probability of any outcome in each lottery and prefers the lottery with the smaller minimum probability. In this case, Lottery B has a minimum probability of 1%, while Lottery A has a minimum probability of 100%, so the reason favors B. The individual in the report also focuses on the 1% probability, downplaying its significance and effectively treating it as irrelevant. While the individual does not explicitly compare minimum probabilities across lotteries, the attention to the 1% outcome and its dismissal suggests an implicit recognition of it as a low-probability event. However, the reasoning is more about ignoring a bad outcome than comparing structural features (minimum probabilities) of the lotteries. The justification is outcome-focused (avoiding loss) rather than rule-based (comparing min probabilities). Therefore, while there is thematic overlap in attending to the 1% probability, the individual’s reasoning does not align with the decision rule’s comparative logic. It is more consistent with a 'probability neglect' or 'wishful thinking' heuristic than with the formal minimum-probability comparison.",
